    "evidence_text": "However, a defined biological role for 2\u2032-O-methylation of mRNA remains elusive.\n\nBacterial RNA and small antiviral compounds activate caspase-1 through cryopyrin/Nalp3\n\n4).\n\nHere we show the effect of cryopyrin deficiency on inflammasome function and immune responses.\n\nKey role of Ubc5 and lysine-63 polyubiquitination in viral activation of IRF3.\n\nMAVS transduces signals from the cytosolic RIG-I-like receptors, which bind to viral RNAs.\n\nThe activation of IRF3 also requires two ubiquitin-binding domains of NEMO.\n\nLoss of DExD/H box RNA helicase LGP2 manifests disparate antiviral responses.\n\nCOMMD proteins, a novel family of structural and functional homologs of MURR1.\n\nThese proteins form multimeric complexes and were identified in a biochemical screen for MURR1-associated factors.\n\nCytosolic viral sensor RIG-I is a 5'-triphosphate-dependent translocase on double-stranded RNA.\n\nTwo N-terminal caspase activation and recruitment domains (CARDs) transmit the signal, and the regulatory domain prevents signaling in the absence of viral RNA.\n\nThis functional integration of two RNA molecular patterns may provide a means to specifically sense and counteract replicating viruses.\n\nThis dialog may be affected by the individual's genetic background.\n\nReal-time RT-PCR, western blot and viability assays were performed to characterize gene/protein expression and viability.\n\nPIC increased MDA5 and PTPN2 mRNA expression, which was inhibited by the specific siRNAs.\n\nThese findings indicate that changes in MDA5 and PTPN2 expression modify beta-cell responses to dsRNA.\n\nMDA5 regulates inflammatory signals, whereas PTPN2 may function as a defence mechanism against pro-apoptotic signals generated by dsRNA.\n\nRIG-I-mediated antiviral responses to single-stranded RNA bearing 5'-phosphates.",
